let's bring in fox news senior strategic  analyst retired general jack keane  general thanks for being here  your thoughts on this day  well it's certainly an astounding day  and in terms of what's taking place  before our eyes and and i think from uh  from the perspective of the military the  american people our soldiers you know  who participate in afghanistan it's also  a humiliating day  um and and very frustrating  there's been some serious  miscalculations made here  brett i mean just a few weeks ago the  president was claiming that the taliban  cannot take over the country the afghan  security forces are going to be able to  stand up against it and obviously what  what's unfolded before our eyes is quite  the opposite and there there are  reasonable explanations for why that is  the case why  why is the situation collapsing so  quickly and one of the intangibles  that's so difficult to measure by our  intelligence community or even our  military leaders  is  what would be the impact of taking  decisive air power away from the afghan  security forces who are used to having  u.s air power and u.s intelligence in  support of them  and they've always with that air power  been able to stop taliban offensives in  the past if we pull that out  what is the impact of that well that's a  subjective evaluation to be sure and  even the military people didn't know  what that impact would have on the  security forces and you can see it  right before our eyes it's not the only  reason but it's the primary reason this  is a ground force that's used to having  very capable u.s  air support when there's an offensive  taking place and they don't have it now  they're coming from other countries but  they call it over the horizon but listen  eight hours to get to the target brett  they can't stay that long when you're  dealing with an offensive you have to  have enduring consistent air power  staying on the target continuously for  hours and that makes the enemy go away  and we need ground coordinators to make  that air power general effect there are  a lot of people who say that's part of  the reason this is a long time this is  almost two decades on the ground and i  did many stories on in afghanistan about  the afghan training and the efforts to  get that force up and running the the  money that was spent the equipment that  was sent to them and the afghan forces  number  a significant number and they have good  equipment but yet  we're saying that they can't defend  themselves we're seeing these cities  fall one after another herat ghazni  kandahar this afternoon and maybe kabul  although there's some thought that maybe  they're not going to do the big fight in  kabul and just wait to negotiate  yes certainly i mean  and that's a valid point to make  we've been there 20 years we're there  because we're involved in a  multi-generational war against radical  islam that doesn't have an end date that  i'm aware of but certainly we have  national leaders that have determined  that as to the forces themselves the  afghan security forces listen we've  always had problems with it we've never  said  that this is a highly capable force that  i'm that i'm aware of but nonetheless we  believe we were training to be effective  enough  with the enablers provided to them  to be able to deal with the taliban we  knew full well they couldn't defeat the  taliban i mean we didn't have enough  forces there to do that ourselves uh  when we were there so but we knew for a  fact that the taliban didn't have the  capability to take the country over  either and it was stalemated and that's  why people were seeking you know a  political solution to what appeared to  be  somewhat of an intractable problem for  both sides that that wasn't the answer  either obviously yeah you know uh  general you serve in the vietnam war  you're a ranger paratrooper uh platoon  leader leader company commander  today  that image of vietnam came up in the  questioning of admiral john kirby take a  listen  are you going to avoid  a parallel with the fall of saigon  what this is going to look like is what  it is we're not walking away from our  commitments to the afghan forces we're  not completely eliminating our  diplomatic presence on the ground we're  still going to have diplomats there  nobody is abandoning afghanistan it's  not walking away from it  it's doing the right thing at the right  time to protect our people  your thoughts on the comparison the  contrasts and where we are today  well certainly it is a reason for a  comparison certainly um  we did lose political uh support and  resolve for the war in vietnam which  finally you know the the congress  terminated any financial support so we  can no longer provide air power to  support uh the ground forces which were  the south vietnamese and what what we're  seeing  right before our eyes today happened  then the north vietnamese rolled it up  very quickly  i i was just it was like a gut punch you  know for a platoon leader company  commander to see an ending like that um  i really held my uh my senior military  leaders and and somewhat in contempt to  be frank about it they're having a wrong  strategy for three years from 65 to 68  which protracted the war and for my  national leaders not having the stomach  the stand up to what was taking place  and and here we we've made similar  miscalculations in my in my mind  listen  we have five places brett and i think we  have evolved over the global war on  terror we've gotten more sophisticated  about it there's 40 to 50 movements out  there we're only involved in five  syria iraq yemen east africa and  afghanistan was one of them this is  where u.s troops are involved for one  reason only because all five of those  places we believe  that the radicals there have aspirations  to attack the american people i only  have a few seconds  but let me just ask and it's a modest  force for all of that and it seems to me  to be a prudent decision and how you're  going to secure the american people just  a few seconds do you think the trump  administration would do something  different in exiting this than the biden  administration has done  well i'm not even convinced that  president trump would eventually have  exited it even though it obviously was  part of his campaign but i mean i can't  do anything but speculate about that  i i don't know for sure uh listen i i do  know when you made the decision to leave  brett there should have been a lot of  other  issues on the table what are we going to  do to prevent the taliban from taking  over because you can't convince me to  president biden  has any interest in the taliban running  afghanistan with all the geopolitical uh  significance that has in dealing with  our adversaries and our allies certainly  that's not on his agenda so why what was  the thoughts in the process how do we  prevent that from happening  does  31 august really make sense and right  how are we going to apportion the  resources i don't know if those  questions really got answered  or even asked for that matter because  this is a huge miscalculation yeah and  there's no sense the taliban wants  international recognition uh from the un  or anybody else uh general jack keane we  appreciate your analysis always  yeah great talking to brett

